{"id":"delayed-label-monitoring","t":"Windowed accuracy monitor with delayed labels","tags":["MLOps","Time Series","Metrics & Evaluation"],"d":"Hard","rt":"pandas","fn":"accuracy_monitor","tol":[1e-06,1e-08],"pkgs":["pandas"],"st":"Ground truth arrives late: a fraud label after a chargeback, a churn label a month later. A production accuracy dashboard must only use labels that had **actually arrived** by the time it's computed, report how much of the window is labelled, and not alert on a handful of labels.\n\nWrite `accuracy_monitor(events, check_times, window, min_labels, floor)` that returns one row per (check time, model).\n\n## Inputs\n- `events`: DataFrame with columns `model` (str), `pred_ts` (int, when the prediction was made), `label_ts` (float: when its label arrived, `NaN` if it hasn't and never will) and `correct` (int 0/1; meaningless while unlabelled).\n- `check_times`: list of ints, possibly unsorted, possibly with repeats. Keep their order.\n- `window` (int > 0), `min_labels` (int >= 1), `floor` (float).\n\n## For a check time $T$ and a model\n- **window events**: that model's events with $T - \\text{window} < $ `pred_ts` $\\le T$.\n- **labelled**: window events with `label_ts <= T`.\n\n| column | dtype | value |\n|---|---|---|\n| `check_ts` | int | $T$ |\n| `model` | str | the model |\n| `n_preds` | int | number of window events |\n| `n_labeled` | int | number of labelled window events |\n| `coverage` | float | `n_labeled / n_preds`, `NaN` if `n_preds == 0` |\n| `accuracy` | float | mean of `correct` over labelled events, `NaN` if `n_labeled == 0` |\n| `alert` | bool | `n_labeled >= min_labels` and `accuracy < floor` |\n\n## Output\nColumns in the order above. One row for **every** model appearing anywhere in `events` at every check time, even with no events in the window. Rows ordered by position in `check_times`, then by `model` ascending. The index is ignored.","starter":"import pandas as pd\n\ndef accuracy_monitor(events, check_times, window, min_labels, floor):\n    \"\"\"One row per (check time, model): check_ts, model, n_preds, n_labeled, coverage, accuracy, alert.\"\"\"\n    rows = []\n    for T in check_times:\n        w = events[(events['pred_ts'] > T - window) & (events['pred_ts'] <= T)]\n        for m, g in w.groupby('model'):                       # labels from the future, missing models\n            acc = g['correct'].mean()\n            rows.append([T, m, len(g), len(g), 1.0, acc, acc < floor])\n    return pd.DataFrame(rows, columns=['check_ts', 'model', 'n_preds', 'n_labeled', 'coverage', 'accuracy', 'alert'])\n","hints":["Handle one check time at a time. The window is `(T - window, T]` on `pred_ts`; inside it, an event is labelled only if `label_ts <= T`. Comparisons with NaN are `False`, so never-labelled events drop out by themselves.","`groupby('model')` only produces models that have events in the window. Compute the model list once from all of `events` (sorted) and `reindex(models, fill_value=0)` each count, so every model gets a row at every check time.","Count `n_labeled` and correct-and-labelled events, then derive coverage and accuracy with `np.where` to get NaN on zero denominators. The alert needs both conditions; make sure a NaN accuracy never alerts."],"sol":"import numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\n\nCOLS = ['check_ts', 'model', 'n_preds', 'n_labeled', 'coverage', 'accuracy', 'alert']\n\ndef accuracy_monitor(events, check_times, window, min_labels, floor):\n    models = sorted(events['model'].unique())\n    parts = []\n    for T in check_times:\n        w = events[(events['pred_ts'] > T - window) & (events['pred_ts'] <= T)]\n        labeled = w['label_ts'] <= T                            # NaN compares False: never labelled\n        g = (w.assign(lab=labeled.astype(int), hit=(labeled & (w['correct'] == 1)).astype(int))\n              .groupby('model')[['lab', 'hit']].agg(['size', 'sum']))\n        # reindex: models with no events in this window still get a row (zeros)\n        n_preds = g[('lab', 'size')].reindex(models, fill_value=0).to_numpy()\n        n_lab = g[('lab', 'sum')].reindex(models, fill_value=0).to_numpy()\n        hits = g[('hit', 'sum')].reindex(models, fill_value=0).to_numpy()\n        with np.errstate(invalid='ignore', divide='ignore'):\n            coverage = np.where(n_preds > 0, n_lab / n_preds, np.nan)\n            accuracy = np.where(n_lab > 0, hits / n_lab, np.nan)\n        parts.append(pd.DataFrame({\n            'check_ts': T, 'model': models, 'n_preds': n_preds, 'n_labeled': n_lab,\n            'coverage': coverage, 'accuracy': accuracy,\n            'alert': (n_lab >= min_labels) & (np.nan_to_num(accuracy, nan=np.inf) < floor),\n        }))\n    if not parts:\n        return pd.DataFrame(columns=COLS)\n    out = pd.concat(parts, ignore_index=True)[COLS]\n    return out.astype({'check_ts': 'int64', 'n_preds': 'int64', 'n_labeled': 'int64',\n                       'coverage': 'float64', 'accuracy': 'float64', 'alert': 'bool'})\n","ex_md":"## Why it works\nAt time $T$ the dashboard could only have seen labels with `label_ts <= T`, so filtering on that reproduces what a live system would have shown. Without it, a backfilled dashboard looks better or worse than reality and an alert rule tuned on it fires at the wrong times. Coverage makes the delay visible: an accuracy of 0.95 on 3% coverage means little, especially because early labels are often a biased sample (fast chargebacks are a particular kind of fraud).\n\nReindexing every count to the full model list turns \"no data\" into an explicit row of zeros and NaNs instead of a silently missing row, which is how monitoring systems distinguish \"healthy\" from \"not reporting\". The alert needs `min_labels` so a single wrong label doesn't page anyone.\n\n## Complexity\n$O(|C| \\cdot n)$ for $|C|$ check times over $n$ events with the per-check filter. For many check times, sort by `pred_ts` and use `searchsorted` to find each window, or bucket events into fixed intervals and pre-aggregate.\n\n## Common bugs\n- Using `correct` for events whose label hasn't arrived yet (the starter): the dashboard \"knows\" the future.\n- Dropping models without events in a window, so a model that stopped serving traffic looks fine (test 2).\n- Window boundaries: `pred_ts == T - window` is outside, `pred_ts == T` is inside (test 4).\n- An alert that fires on `NaN < floor` evaluated as truthy somewhere, or on coverage too low to mean anything.\n\n## Interview follow-ups\n- Labels take two weeks. What can you monitor in the meantime that correlates with accuracy? (Prediction score distribution, input drift such as PSI, confidence, proxy labels like early user feedback.)\n- How would you correct the bias that early-arriving labels are not representative? (Compare against the delay distribution from history, report accuracy per label-delay bucket, or reweight by the probability of a label having arrived.)","nex":2,"shown":[{"in":"  model  pred_ts  label_ts  correct\n0    m1        1       2.0        1\n1    m1        3       4.0        0\n2    m1        5      20.0        1\n[5, 25]\n10\n1\n0.6","out":"   check_ts model  n_preds  n_labeled  coverage  accuracy  alert\n0         5    m1        3          2  0.666667       0.5   True\n1        25    m1        0          0       NaN       NaN  False"},{"in":"  model  pred_ts  label_ts  correct\n0     a       10      11.0        1\n1     b       10       NaN        0\n2     b       12      13.0        0\n[12, 9]\n5\n1\n0.5","out":"   check_ts model  n_preds  n_labeled  coverage  accuracy  alert\n0        12     a        1          1       1.0       1.0  False\n1        12     b        2          0       0.0       NaN  False\n2         9     a        0          0       NaN       NaN  False\n3         9     b        0          0       NaN       NaN  False"}],"lessons":[{"href":"/self-learning-tracks/courses/system-design-101/media-and-operations-designs/observability-and-slos/","t":"Observability and SLOs","c":"System Design"},{"href":"/self-learning-tracks/courses/inference-eng-course/observability-reliability-security/inference-metrics-prometheus-grafana/","t":"Inference Metrics, Prometheus & Grafana","c":"Inference Engineering"},{"href":"/self-learning-tracks/courses/machine-learning/ml-in-production/monitoring-models/","t":"Monitoring models","c":"Machine Learning"},{"href":"/self-learning-tracks/courses/ml-toolkit/pandas-for-ml-data/time-series-in-pandas/","t":"Time series in pandas","c":"ML Toolkit"}],"related":["consecutive-breach-alerts","expected-calibration-error"],"ntests":8,"tj":"[{\"a\": [{\"__df__\": {\"columns\": [\"model\", \"pred_ts\", \"label_ts\", \"correct\"], \"data\": [[\"m1\", 1, 2.0, 1], [\"m1\", 3, 4.0, 0], [\"m1\", 5, 20.0, 1]]}, \"dtypes\": [\"object\", \"int64\", \"float64\", \"int64\"]}, [5, 25], 10, 1, 0.6], \"e\": {\"__df__\": {\"columns\": [\"check_ts\", \"model\", \"n_preds\", \"n_labeled\", \"coverage\", \"accuracy\", \"alert\"], \"data\": [[5, \"m1\", 3, 2, 0.6666666666666666, 0.5, true], [25, \"m1\", 0, 0, {\"__f__\": \"nan\"}, {\"__f__\": \"nan\"}, false]]}, \"dtypes\": [\"int64\", \"object\", \"int64\", \"int64\", \"float64\", \"float64\", \"bool\"]}}, {\"a\": [{\"__df__\": {\"columns\": [\"model\", \"pred_ts\", \"label_ts\", \"correct\"], \"data\": [[\"a\", 10, 11.0, 1], [\"b\", 10, {\"__f__\": \"nan\"}, 0], [\"b\", 12, 13.0, 0]]}, \"dtypes\": [\"object\", \"int64\", \"float64\", \"int64\"]}, [12, 9], 5, 1, 0.5], \"e\": {\"__df__\": {\"columns\": [\"check_ts\", \"model\", \"n_preds\", \"n_labeled\", \"coverage\", \"accuracy\", \"alert\"], \"data\": [[12, \"a\", 1, 1, 1.0, 1.0, false], [12, \"b\", 2, 0, 0.0, {\"__f__\": \"nan\"}, false], [9, \"a\", 0, 0, {\"__f__\": \"nan\"}, {\"__f__\": \"nan\"}, false], [9, \"b\", 0, 0, {\"__f__\": \"nan\"}, {\"__f__\": \"nan\"}, false]]}, \"dtypes\": [\"int64\", \"object\", \"int64\", \"int64\", \"float64\", \"float64\", \"bool\"]}}, {\"a\": [{\"__df__\": {\"columns\": [\"model\", \"pred_ts\", \"